  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday through Sunday)\nIssued at 149 AM MDT Mon Oct 19 2026\n\nAn active and wet Fall pattern is forecast mid-week through at least \nSaturday. A closed Low is progged to break off from the subtropical \njet stream Tuesday, settling off the California Coast Wednesday. At \nthe same time, ridging will amplify over the Central Plains, \nfocusing a moisture plume over the desert southwest.\n\nVery strong southerly moisture transport, as evidenced by IVT values \napproaching 600 units (kg/ms), will set up over central AZ \nWednesday, shifting into eastern AZ and western NM on Thursday as \nthe closed Low slides eastward. The main area of uncertainty over \nthe past 2 days has revolved around the track of the Low as it moves \ninland into the Intermountain West. Ensemble guidance has trended \nmore progressive with its movement inland, with only a small \nminority (~15%) of ensembles still showing a near-stationary closed \nLow over soCal.\n\nLarge scale ascent will create large swaths of precipitation late \nWednesday through Friday, with stronger bands focusing around the \nvort max that will eject across the Four Corners region Thursday. \nRainfall totals have ticked higher for the Four Corners, with 50th \npercentile amounts over the 48 hour period from Wednesday morning \nthrough Friday morning now showing 1.8\" in Gallup and 2.1\" in \nFarmington. Notably, the average recurrence interval (ARI) for these \n50th percentile 48 hour rainfall amounts range from 4 years in \nGallup to 20 years in Farmington. Given that synoptic scale features \nwill drive much of this precipitation, the spread of rainfall \namounts (e.g. 10th to 90th percentile) is on the lower side compared \nto a typical monsoonal surge. Forecast instability remains highest \nover southeast Arizona and southern New Mexico Thursday then over \nsoutheast New Mexico on Friday where clearing should promote more \nsurface heating. This will be something to watch closely as it could \nbe the case that the flash flood threat is not necessarily co-\nlocated with the highest forecast rainfall amounts.\n\nBeyond Friday, model disagreement remains high, but the overall \nforecast continues to trend wetter. The jet stream is forecast to \nremain north over the northern Rockies, but some models want to re-\ndevelop another Low off the coast of California, which would keep \nmoist southerly flow over New Mexico through the weekend into the \nearly part of next week. The majority of guidance (~75%) of models \nfeature a slower developing longwave trough over The Great Basin, \nalthough those solutions are not advertised by current deterministic \nguidance.",
